WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.750
Welcome back to Curious Archive.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.810
This video is the third part of a series exploring
the incredible speculative evolution project

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.500
of Serina, the world of birds.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.500
Serina was created by Dylan Bajda — a terrific
artist and worldbuilder.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.060
I have links below where you can follow and
support his work.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.200
When we left off, Serina was home to a single
supercontinent at the end of the Pangeacene.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.940
Now it is the Ultimocene, the final era of
Serina — and one more incredible than any

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.700
that came before.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:37.399
At 250 million years post-establishment, the
supercontinent has split into northern and

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:38.970
southern landmasses.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.290
This period will see the rise of multiple
forms of highly intelligent life.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:47.970
And in much the same way that elephants, dolphins,
crows, and humans — which as far as we know

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:52.929
are some of the most intelligent animals ever
to exist — all happen to live in an overlapping

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:58.010
time period, a similar convergence will occur
on Serina.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.120
On the enormous grassland of the southern
continent, many key species can be found:

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.400
like the successful Circuagodonts, who look
unrecognizable from their roots as tiny fish

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.910
250 million years ago.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:16.330
Giant Archangels flock nearby, a type of metamorph
bird — a curious classification that will

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.690
define the Early Ultimocene.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:24.370
And scavenging close by, a large bumblebager
makes off with a young Circuagodont — foreshadowing

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:28.020
a rivalry between the two groups that will
change Serina forever...

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.200
But we’re not there yet.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.110
Right now, softbilled birds, the varied descendants
of ancient water snuffles, are prospering

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.130
during the Ultimocene.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:41.250
Organisms like the bludgebirds are now extinct,
but another offshoot of the mittens, the southern

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.790
squid stork (or squork), are still going strong.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.630
These intelligent creatures are omnivores,
and use their tentacle-beaks to grab hold

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.720
of hidden worms and mollusks.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.560
In temperate forests, another softbilled bird
is the shy and reclusive gmu (moo), a horse-sized

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.530
herbivore that lives perpetually on alert.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.140
Why would a creature of this size be so skittish?

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.670
The truth is, it has good reason to be.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:09.349
Elsewhere in these forests lurk the Grappler
— predators similar in build to the gmu

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:11.189
(moo), which are their preferred prey.

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:15.439
Their tentacle-beaks are lined with sharp
teeth and tipped with talons, making them

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:17.159
deadly apex predators.

00:02:17.159 --> 00:02:22.050
A different branch of softbills are the terries,
a group that includes the hilariously-named

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:27.560
noots — whose facial-tentacles fused over
time into one elongated, fleshy mouth.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.879
When threatened, these birds are able to scoop
up their egg and carry it against their chins,

00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:36.079
where a small fold of feathered skin keeps
it secure while they flee.

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:41.349
A related group are the neckbeards, who like
the noots have an amusing name and carry their

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:45.720
eggs against their chins — although the
neckbeard takes this new extreme by having

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.530
a more defined pouch to hold their eggs: allowing
them to stop brooding on the ground at all.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.439
In warmer forests, you can find the Lumberbeests,
the largest living mucks on all of Serina

00:02:55.439 --> 00:03:01.430
— standing up to twenty-five feet, or 7.6
meters tall, and weighing over two tons.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:07.510
This group, however, is in the decline: with
most now wandering the forests in isolation.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.489
In the branches above, however, life flourishes.

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:15.980
Here, much-changed tree-dwelling tribbets
swing near the bizarre, tentacled boras — an

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.469
offshoot of the softbilled mittens.

00:03:19.469 --> 00:03:20.920
Fantastic forms of life indeed.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:26.360
On the subject of fantastic forms, possibly
no creatures are more unique than the changelings,

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.189
or metamorph birds.

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:32.310
Although metamorphs reach their peak in the
early Ultimocene, their ancestry goes all

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.290
the way back to the Cryocene.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:40.060
The metamorph line began with birds that laid
small, shell-less eggs, which made the process

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.560
faster but led to their chicks being born
extremely underdeveloped — almost looking

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.650
like bird tadpoles before they reached adulthood.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:54.010
Over millions of years, this larva-like stage
became more complex, with the tiny young developing

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:58.540
stronger forelimbs to move around more effectively
before becoming adults.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.040
Other metamorph embryos were laid in the water
and became more fish-like, adapting for life

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.219
in an aquatic environment.

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:09.700
Millions of years on, the end result is perhaps
the most adaptable and physically diverse

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.239
vertebrate clade (cl-aid) to ever exist.

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:18.849
Among their ranks are Zebra tweezle, bumblebee-sized
birds that begin life as worm-like larva and

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:21.519
are among the smallest birds ever.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.820
Another metamorph is the blue-throated boomsinger:
one of the largest birds ever to walk the

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:30.819
land which stands up to fifty feet, or 15
and a quarter meters tall.

00:04:30.819 --> 00:04:35.150
Some aquatic metamorphs like the Longbilled
Pikebird have even abandoned their adult forms

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.729
all together through an extreme expression
of neoteny — essentially becoming fish-like

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:41.729
organisms.

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:42.729
Truly incredible.

00:04:42.729 --> 00:04:47.599
Other metamorph birds have a somewhat unfortunate
lot in life, like the Dayflight Bird.

00:04:47.599 --> 00:04:51.300
These extreme metamorphs begin as incredibly
fish-like larvae.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:56.199
They then cocoon themselves in a silken wrapping
of their own saliva, which hardens into what

00:04:56.199 --> 00:05:00.860
is effectively a second egg — within which
they metamorphize into adults.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:04.449
Tragically, however, the adults lack mouths.

00:05:04.449 --> 00:05:10.460
The dayflights therefore live only a single
day, during which they lay eggs to repeat

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.590
the cycle.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.990
And if this sounds too out-of-the-box to evolve,
know that real-world Mayfly insects have a

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:20.990
very similar lifecycle that ends in an adult
form that lacks a mouth.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:25.550
Perhaps the most impressive metamorph bird,
however, is the The Stormsonor — a highly

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:32.139
migratory archangel with a wingspan of fifty
feet, or over 15 meters: the largest flying

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:34.530
animals ever to exist.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.750
Despite being larger than some airplanes,
Stormsonors are gentle giants, named for their

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.009
eerie, echoing calls.

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:47.120
As previously stated, aside from the metamorph
birds, one of the major themes of the Ultimocene

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.930
is the development of intelligence among many
animal lineages.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:55.889
In the oceans, the herbivorous Ringnecked
Porplets and carnivorous Seastrikers are two

00:05:55.889 --> 00:05:57.270
such examples.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.250
Both species are social animals with keen
minds.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:04.990
The Seastrikers have learned to operate like
sheepdogs, systematically herding the porplets

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.259
to create a consistent food source.

00:06:07.259 --> 00:06:12.479
Considering how advanced — and similar — both
species are, it’s an unsettling system,

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:15.379
proving nature can be quite cruel.

00:06:15.379 --> 00:06:18.599
And nothing is quite as cruel as an ending.

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:23.440
In the words of the author: “as happens
to everything in the cosmos from flowers to

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.699
stars, Serina itself is dying.”

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:31.550
The moon is starting to slowly cool down,
and it will never warm up again.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:36.620
As stated before, the Ultimocene will be the
final era in the saga of Serina, the last

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:37.620
chapter in our great epic…

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.810
But before the curtain falls on Serina’s
finale, there may still be time for something

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.449
truly incredible to occur.

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:50.120
Over the past several million years, one branch
of the Circuagodonts has begun an extraordinary

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.210
transformation.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:56.120
For eons, Circuagodonts have been unable to
feed on tall trees due to their short necks.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.759
But now, one species has specialized a body
part that will allow them to do so.

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:03.090
Not their necks, but their ears.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:07.979
From their hearing structure, Circuagodonts
have developed antler-like mobile limbs that

00:07:07.979 --> 00:07:10.479
are able to reach up and hook branches.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:15.400
An adaptation which might one day have intriguing
consequences.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:19.960
Before that day comes, however, other branches
of the tribbetheres are also doing pretty

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:24.569
well for themselves — rivaling the birds
in diversity several millions of years after

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:26.300
crawling onto land.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:30.960
Take the painted repandor: the widest-spread
canithere on land in the early Ultimocene,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.069
whose jaws have a unique shearing motion that
helps them consume prey.

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:41.319
Or the thorngrazer, a hairless Molodont able
to survive off bristly desert plants alone.

00:07:41.319 --> 00:07:46.849
In the skies, the branch that became the Tribbats
is also going strong: like the Blue-eared

00:07:46.849 --> 00:07:51.860
Vibropteryx, a nectar-drinking specialist
that uses a long brush-tipped tongue to gather

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:52.879
food.

00:07:52.879 --> 00:07:57.800
And in the seas, yet another offshoot is becoming
aquatic — like the Spotted Snailsmashers,

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.050
a strange looking group that feeds on various
types of mollusks.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:08.009
Between the metamorph birds and various tribbetheres,
Serina is at an all-time high of biodiversity.

00:08:08.009 --> 00:08:11.520
But now the moon is getting truly cold.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.979
We are now in the Middle Ultimocene, and a
new ice age is setting in.

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:20.550
In this new world, only the most adaptable
will survive.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:26.210
Sextacorn Thorngazers are one such species,
which have become thick coated ice age herbivores

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:30.599
with specialized facial horns that give them
a menacing look.

00:08:30.599 --> 00:08:35.469
Despite appearances, these creatures aren’t
overly aggressive, just interested in self-defense

00:08:35.469 --> 00:08:37.610
from ice age predators.

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:42.039
Another curious-looking new arrival in the
ice age are mammoth trunkos, descendants of

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:46.750
the neckbeards that have now developed both
a longer beak-trunk and a truly robust neck-pouch

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:51.089
to carry their eggs in — which has come
in handy in the cooling temperatures.

00:08:51.089 --> 00:08:56.450
At this point in the ice age, the largest
archangels are sadly in trouble, as the rapidly

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:01.220
cooling world is making their migratory patterns
less and less effective.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:06.320
Some archangels are adapting, however, by
becoming smaller and less migratory offshoots

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.300
called Seraphs.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:12.410
Examples include the Ptundra Pteese, which
might not be as spectacular as their larger

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:16.279
relatives, but are much better adapted for
this new world.

00:09:16.279 --> 00:09:22.390
Closer to the equator, a story unlike any
other is now at last set to begin.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:26.959
Over the past several million years, a group
of Circuagodonts called the Antleers, which

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:33.320
have become more intelligent with better antler-like
manipulator appendages, find themselves blindsided

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:39.029
by a creature that hunts them with an astonishing
degree of planning and foresight.

00:09:39.029 --> 00:09:43.590
These lifeforms, the Gravediggers, are a descendant
of the bumblebadgers.

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:49.300
While other predators evolved sharper teeth
or longer claws to catch their prey, the gravedigger

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.500
has developed a bigger brain.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:56.250
They have, incredibly, learned how to make
traps by digging deep pits into the earth

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:01.140
with their powerful armclaws, and using their
beaks to sharpen sticks which they place at

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:02.140
the bottom.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:05.660
It’s a kind of trap the Antleers are helpless
against.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:07.130
But not for long.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:12.470
By catching the less wary antleers, the gravediggers
are inadvertently selecting for smarter and

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:13.839
smarter prey.

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:17.209
And so the evolutionary arms race is set into
motion.

00:10:17.209 --> 00:10:23.140
It will be perhaps the most rapid of any so
far on Serina — as well as the most pivotal.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:27.720
Soon, one particular type of Antleer — the
standing Antleer, will become intelligent

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:33.190
enough to avoid traps: and therefore produce
gravediggers with more effective trapping

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.630
abilities.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:39.940
In time, the antleers and gravediggers both
achieve sapience, able to use basic tools

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:42.570
and avoid each other.

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:48.050
While no longer predator and prey, a hatred
had risen between both groups that would define

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:51.550
the coming era in unexpected ways.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:56.329
The Standing Antleers have now become the
Woodcrafters, one-of-a-kind looking social

00:10:56.329 --> 00:11:02.639
organisms with highly expressive faces for
communication and exceptionally advanced brains.

00:11:02.639 --> 00:11:06.520
Woodcrafters have also developed a whistling
language somewhat similar to the bugles of

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:12.310
an elk, and use spears to defend against predators
— although they themselves are herbivores.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.810
Spears aren’t the only things they’ve
learned to craft, however.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:20.730
As their name implies, Woodcrafters have learned
to shape the trunks and branches of trees

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:26.079
into basket-like houses — as well as fences,
bridges over rivers, or sculptures of beasts

00:11:26.079 --> 00:11:28.680
or abstract creatures from lore.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:33.760
For the Woodcrafters are developing a culture
— one that is in large part defined by a

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.769
hatred of predators.

00:11:35.769 --> 00:11:39.620
Meanwhile, the Gravediggers are achieving
a similar level of brain-function.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:44.640
While their marks on the world are subtler
than the woodcrafters — often little more

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:49.310
than art carved into the sides of trees at
the edge of their territory — they are still

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.019
marks of high intelligence.

00:11:51.019 --> 00:11:54.990
However, the gravediggers are gloomily learning
to be careful when they make these marks,

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.709
as it can attract dangers...

00:11:57.709 --> 00:12:01.910
The Woodcrafters, which have at this point
driven most predators from their environment

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:06.670
on a crusade to make a safer world, are now
leaving their territory in search of more

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:08.560
predators to slay.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.899
Once the prey of gravediggers, the Woodcrafter
have turned the tables entirely.

00:12:12.899 --> 00:12:18.790
Instead of acting in self-defense, they are
now killing gravediggers in a misplaced crusade.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:22.750
Eventually, however, an incredible transformation
occurs.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:27.880
As they expand out, Woodcrafters begin to
encounter barrier trees made by Gravediggers

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:29.910
who never learned to stop making art.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:36.490
At first, the Woodcrafters assume these markings
are the work of some unfamiliar Woodcrafter

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:37.550
village.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:42.399
After all, in their worldview, only they are
intelligent enough to create art.

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:47.529
But there are no signs of others…

00:12:47.529 --> 00:12:52.440
In the words of the author: ‘The woodcrafters
had set out to create a world without monsters,

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.620
and it is only then that they realize that
they have become them.”

00:12:56.620 --> 00:13:00.920
With the revelation that they are not alone
at their arbitrary pinnacle of life: that

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:06.740
the monstrous gravediggers they’d slain
for years are intelligent just like they ares,

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.060
spreads through the woodcrafter groups.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:14.110
And it is at this time of cultural uncertainty
that the trajectory of the two species shifts

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.070
forever from a small choice.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:20.720
An orphaned gravedigger infant, left alone
after the death of their mother — one of

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:26.920
the last gravediggers hunted for sport — is
taken in by an empathetic woodcrafter warrior-to-be.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:31.850
This gravedigger child, aptly referred to
as ‘Bridge,’ is at first regarded with

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.199
suspicion by the other Woodcrafters.

00:13:34.199 --> 00:13:39.029
Soon, however, they’d learn to socialize
with the group, displaying a personality and

00:13:39.029 --> 00:13:42.630
emotional complexity normally hidden from
outsiders.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:47.279
Bridge would forge a path for cooperation
between the two most intelligent species on

00:13:47.279 --> 00:13:48.279
Serina.

00:13:48.279 --> 00:13:52.699
The woodcrafters and the gravediggers, intertwined
since their shared beginning — would break

00:13:52.699 --> 00:13:58.290
a multi-million-year cycle of violence between
intelligent predator and intelligent prey.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:03.079
In time, other young Gravediggers following
Bridge’s example would abandon their territorial

00:14:03.079 --> 00:14:08.440
ways and imprint on Woodcrafters, and the
woodcrafters would in turn accept the Gravediggers

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:09.870
into their society.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:15.459
Eventually, the younger generations would
never know a world where the antleers and

00:14:15.459 --> 00:14:16.459
the gravedigger were enemies.

00:14:16.459 --> 00:14:19.400
And whatever the future would hold, the two
species would face it together.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:20.400
So, what comes next?

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:26.500
Perhaps only tragedy awaits all life on Serina
as it continues to cool, but it’s always

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:32.650
been a saga of both the good and the bad — a
story of life and death, of extinction and

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:33.650
evolution.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:37.579
In any case, for the time being, one thing
is for sure: the mudwickets and the bumblets

00:14:37.579 --> 00:14:39.699
sure came a long way.

00:14:39.699 --> 00:14:43.910
Dylan Bajda has released some content set
after this period, but I don’t want to dive

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:47.740
further into the future just yet until the
whole project has been unveiled.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:51.089
So it’s a story which will have to be told
another time.

00:14:51.089 --> 00:14:53.949
For now, this is the end of our three-part
series.

00:14:53.949 --> 00:14:58.310
If you find this speculative evolution epic
as enthralling as I did, please follow and

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:05.370
support Dylan Bajada and his project using
the links below.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:12.930
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:17.740
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:20.740
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:21.959
See you in the next video.

